        <ArticleTitle>Transoral robot-assisted lingual tonsillectomy and uvulopalatopharyngoplasty for obstructive sleep apnea.</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>635-9</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We assessed the use of transoral robot-assisted lingual tonsillectomy and uvulopalatopharyngoplasty for the surgical management of tongue base obstruction in patients with obstructive sleep apnea.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In a prospective, nonrandomized trial using historical controls, patients underwent drug-induced sleep endoscopy, transoral robot-assisted lingual tonsillectomy with uvulopalatopharyngoplasty, and preoperative and postoperative polysomnography.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty patients have completed the study to date. The rate of surgical success was 45%, and the rate of surgical response was 65%. The mean preoperative apnea-hypopnea index of 55.6 decreased by 56.7%, to a mean postoperative value of 24.1 (p &lt; 0.001), and the minimum arterial oxygen saturation increased from the mean preoperative value of 75.8% to the mean postoperative value of 81.7% (p = 0.013). The mean Epworth Sleepiness Scale score improved from 13.4 to 5.9 (p = 0.003). One patient had postoperative bleeding that required cauterization, resulting in a major complication rate of 4.2%.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Transoral robot-assisted lingual tonsillectomy with uvulopalatopharyngoplasty is a novel technique for the surgical management of obstructive sleep apnea that results in a significant decrease in the apnea-hypopnea index, a significant improvement in minimum arterial oxygen saturation, and a significant improvement in the Epworth Sleepiness Scale score and has an acceptable complication rate.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
